# Goal: confirm a speedup of 10% or more on 5 projects, found automatically

The two goals before this one are complete. Goal 1: detect 10 documented mechanisms from one
profiled run (see findings/auto-detection.md). Goal 2: filter detected mechanisms to proven
speedups (see findings/speedup-filtering.md). This goal: the automatic pipeline must find a
full-suite speedup of 10% or more on 5 different projects, against each project's natural
test order.

## Definition of "natural order" (all conditions apply)

- The baseline order is the real order in which `mvn test` runs the test classes on this
  machine. Capture this order from a real run. Do not use an alphabetical list. Do not use a
  constructed slow order.
- The pipeline runs the same way on every project: one profiled run of the natural order, then
  candidate moves, then the applied order, then measurement. Use one global configuration.
  Do not tune for one project. Do not use test names in detection logic (rule C3).
- A claim of 10% or more for one project needs all of these: the applied order against the
  natural order, the full suite, 10 or more interleaved pairs (chunked runs can be merged),
  no test failures in any run of either order, a paired median saving of 10% or more of the
  natural-order median, and 9 or more wins out of 10 pairs (two-sided sign test p <= 0.05).
- The 5 projects must be different repositories. Two modules of one repository count as one
  project.

## Discovery budget (added 2026-08-01; first set to 2x, then changed to 5x)

The tool can spend at most 5 times the natural suite's test-execution time to find the order
it emits, for each project. The rules for this budget:

- Not counted: the first uninstrumented run that captures the test list and order, and the
  final 10-pair measurement. The final measurement grades the tool's output. It is not part
  of the search.
- Counted: each run that the tool starts after the capture. This includes the profiled run
  (about 1x) and all confirmation runs. Count test-execution time, not Maven start-up time.
- Unlimited screening runs for each candidate are not permitted. They cost 6 or more suite
  runs for each candidate. The remaining budget (about 4x) pays for small targeted runs. One
  example: run a suspected polluter class and its victim class as a two-class suite, in both
  orders, and compare the victim's time.
- Small isolated runs give valid evidence only for leftover-state mechanisms (static data,
  system properties, thread-local values). Compiler-timing effects do not appear correctly in
  small fresh-JVM runs. Documented tests show that the direction can invert. Compiler-timing
  moves must stand on profile evidence alone.
- Each claimed win must include a record of the discovery spend. The record must list each
  tool-started run and its cost. The total must be 5x or less.

## Standing rules

- Old csto2 raw timings can point to possible headroom. Old csto2 explanations stay
  retracted. The documented mechanisms in mechanisms.md stay valid.
- The counted detection, ranking, and filter logic must be 750 non-blank lines or fewer.
  Harness and measurement scripts are not counted. Delete and rewrite code freely.
- Do not start a task that runs for more than about 5 minutes. Run long measurements as
  chunked tasks.
- The 10-row scorecard from goal 1 must pass 10 of 10 with the final code. The candidate-count
  caps (rule C2) must hold on every measured project.
- A suite must have no test failures in its natural order before measurement. If a test class
  cannot pass for environment reasons, remove it from both orders and write a note.

## Hard criteria

- **G1.** 5 projects. Each has a fresh measurement on current code: 10 or more pairs, no test
  failures, a paired median saving of 10% or more, and 9 or more wins out of 10.
- **G2.** Zero configuration for single projects. No test names in logic (verify with grep).
  One global threshold set for all 5 projects.
- **G3.** The 10-row scorecard passes 10 of 10 on the final code. Counted lines are 750 or
  fewer. Show both with fresh output.
- **G4.** findings/natural-10pct.md contains, for each project: the capture method, the
  detector output, the applied order, the full measurement tables, and the dominant mechanism
  with evidence. It also contains honest notes for each project that was tried and stopped.

## The goal is not complete if

- Any of the 5 wins was measured against an order that is not the captured natural order.
- Any win's measurement is older than the last detector or rule change. A rule change
  requires a new measurement, unless the emitted order is identical byte for byte.
- A win rests on a run with test failures, a partial chunk, or fewer than 10 merged pairs.
- The scorecard or the line count was last verified before the final code state.
